      "card_ids": ["C12"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Pidotimod, a small acidic amide/thioether molecule, has oral bioavailability of 42–44% in humans, showing that the query's polar acidic features do not preclude F ≥ 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C13"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Ampicillin, a zwitterionic beta-lactam with a carboxylic acid, has oral bioavailability of 29% in fasting female subjects, another acidic small molecule above the threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C07"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Phenylbutyric acid, a small aromatic carboxylic acid, has absolute bioavailability of 78% in humans, supporting absorption of small acids.",
      "card_ids": ["C01"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "current_gold_voting_records",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C07", "C08", "C12", "C13"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement for zofenoprilat itself; the closest ACE-inhibitor active-acid context (C08) reports metabolite availability after prodrug administration, not direct F of the diacid.",
    "The query's low logD (-1.7) and full ionization at pH 7.4 raise permeability concerns, but pH-dependent gastric absorption is possible; no direct permeability or efflux data are supplied.",
    "Transfer from pidotimod, ampicillin, and phenylbutyric acid is limited by structural differences such as absence of a basic amine and different ring systems.",
    "No data on first-pass metabolism or clearance of zofenoprilat are provided."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F ≥ 20%) because all transferable L1 records show oral bioavailability at or above 20% for structurally related small acidic/peptide-like molecules (perindoprilat 30%, perindopril 66–95%, pidotimod 42–44%, ampicillin 29%, phenylbutyric acid 78%), and no affirmative evidence supports F < 20%. The unfavorable logD prior is a permeability concern but not sufficient to overturn the consistent supportive direction; confidence is low due to indirect transfer."
}

